<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chaulukya dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 940–1244</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Solanki dynasty, Chaulukyas of Gujarat</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Chaulukya dynasty, also called the Solankis, ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patan-gujarat">Anahilapataka</a> (Patan) from about 940 to 1244. Under kings such as Bhima I, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayasimha-siddharaja">Jayasimha Siddharaja</a> and Kumarapala it controlled much of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and patronised Jain and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temples, including the sun temple at Modhera. Its feudatories included the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chauhan">Chahamanas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalore">Jalor</a>, and its power passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghel">Vaghelas</a>, a collateral branch.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chaulukya-dynasty.jpg" alt="Chaulukya dynasty" width="320" height="296" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Map created from DEMIS Mapserver, which are public domain. Koba-chan. Reference: [1] · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Chaulukyas.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
